A||N||11||85gt||Headlight Idea||||3||
Z||000000||85gt||04-11-2000||07:55 PM||||Has anyone ever thought of modifying the headlight assy. to use lights like the ones Tom has, and only have the doors open up slightly? I think it would look sharp.||||reg||3
Z||000001||stimpy||04-11-2000||08:21 PM||||Interesting thought... <BR>Now you are talking about the headlights set back in the bucket look, correct? Then have the doors open 3-4" to expose the lights?<BR>Just brainstorming...please don't take this as pissin on your parade, but...<BR>You need to devise some way of actuating the headlight doors since the range of travel of the regular headlight mechanism is blocked by the light buckets. If $ is not a deep consideration, perhaps a hydraulic actuator could be used. This would also keep the wind from ripping the doors off when thewind catches it at speed. Probably would cause a hell of a racket though at speed.<BR>Another thing, With the lights set so far back, the light pattern is rather compromised. Not too bad apparently, but sheilding the top of the light pattern couldn't help.<BR>A couple of alternatives. There is a guy on this forum who has written a very detailed method of making "low-rise" pop-up headlights using the (I think) Camaro headlight. That looks very cool and does not sound really expensive, just need some machining skills to fab some brackets.  Hey guy, if you see this, could you post the link again?<BR>The other idea that yours inspired in me would be to use those hydraulic actuators to push or pull the headlight doors along a track like a garage door...or a sunroof...know what I mean?<BR>Hmmmmmmm...  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||85gt||04-11-2000||08:34 PM||||Actually, I meant haveing the lights like normal light only smaller. All you would have to do is build a bracket to hold the lights. I don't think it would be that hard. Basically it would be a low profile pop-up headlight. The idea with the sunroof style slider sounds good too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||04-11-2000||09:01 PM||||Yeah, the low-rise pop-up has been done, I have seen the pics and they are cool. If I recollect correct, it does require using a glass cutter to modify the sealed beam headlights to use a halogen high-low beam. I think if you search you would eventually find the thread that has the link.<BR>I looked at my car to try to figure out how the sliding ddors would work and I thhink the most workable thing would be to have them slide towards   the midline of the car.  Tom, if you see this, how much clearance is along the top of the headlight in your set in headlights? Enough to run a track?<BR>What do you think, is it do-able?  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||sdm1964||04-11-2000||10:17 PM||||OR how about spin all the way around like a vette?||||reg||14
Z||000005||lawrence||04-11-2000||10:42 PM||||I did this to my car:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/other/fiero/waycool.gif" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/other/fiero/waycool.gif</A> <P>Here's a rough explanation of what I did:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/other/fiero/lowprofile.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/other/fiero/lowprofile.htm</A> <P>It's a very straightfoward project, but it takes a bit of patience.  I can just barely see the tops of the doors from the driver's seat.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||04-11-2000||11:10 PM||||THAT'S who it was! I'm sorry lawrence, I'll try to remember in the future.||||reg||1
Z||000007||TOM||04-11-2000||11:54 PM||||I thought of doing a low profife pop up with me light but have not had the time yto play with it. I dont think it would be had at all||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||04-12-2000||09:47 AM||||Lawrence--I noticed that you have an '88.  Is it possible to modify the motors on a pre-'87?  (I have an '85 GT.)  I'd probably just use the low beam lights with driving lights for highway use.  Feel free to email me if the procedure is lengthy.<BR>Also, tell me about your air dam.  Price, where didja buy it, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Standard||04-12-2000||02:28 PM||||Me too, me too!  I really like how the lo-profile looks.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||lawrence||04-12-2000||05:47 PM||||Don't know anything about the pre-87 headlight motors, but I've heard that they are controlled by limit switches rather than current draw.  You won't be able to put in a stop, as I did with mine.  Maybe if you take one apart, you can modify it in such a way as to read the limit "sooner".  If you are electronically minded, you can probably take my Automatic Power Window Controller and make that work as a headlight motor controller; it's the same principle.<P>For the air dam, I made it myself.  Look at my site and go to the Archives.  I described what I did in the Letters section of one of the issues -- I think the one after the Brake Bleeder article.<P>Hell, I'll just look it up...<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/99/1/tte.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/99/1/tte.htm</A> <P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||04-17-2000||03:05 PM||||lawrence--May I suggest a little money making venture?  Maybe you and TOM can get together on your air dam.  He's awfully good at making a mold of something and reproducing it to sell.  Just a suggestion.<P>Anyone know how much it would cost for a complete '87-'88 headlight motor setup?  How tough it is to swap systems?  I know I'd need to swap the wiring harness, too.||||reg||1
